## =============================================================================
## Core Workflow
## =============================================================================

feat:Ingest
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Ingest" ;
    skos:definition     "Add knowledge from text, URLs, documents, calendars, iCal feeds, Indico events, or Turtle files. An LLM extracts semantic triples (small facts: a subject, a relationship, an object) from unstructured input. Every extracted triple starts as pending for your review." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI .

feat:ReviewSystem
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdf:type            skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Review — where your graph gets smaller and truer" ;
    skos:altLabel       "Review System" ;
    skos:definition     "The step where you decide what is true. A model proposes triples; you confirm, refine or reject each one, and nothing enters your graph without that. It is the slowest part of the product and the only reason the rest of it is worth anything." ;
    kpred:audience      "Anyone weighing this against a notes app, and wondering why they should do the extra work." ;
    kpred:principle     "THE POINT IS NOT GATEKEEPING. IT IS SUPERSEDING. A notepad accumulates: write we are going with Aprimo in March and we chose Bynder in July, and both sentences sit there forever with nothing to reconcile them. Search finds whichever matched your words. A retrieval system indexes both and will happily cite the March one at you in September, with a confident tone and a real quotation. Review is the moment a new fact REPLACES an old one instead of joining it — the superseded statement keeps its place in the record, marked as superseded, and stops being what your graph says." ;
    kpred:principle     "WHICH MEANS THE GRAPH GETS SMALLER AND TRUER OVER TIME. That is the property no append-only store has, and it is the whole return on the work. A year of notes is a year of sediment you must re-read. A year of reviewed facts is a year of decisions you can act on, with the discarded ones still visible if you want to know what you used to think." ;
    kpred:principle     "THE QUEUE SHOWS WHAT CHANGED, NOT EVERYTHING FOUND. New claims, near-duplicates that are probably one thing under two names, and — the valuable ones — claims that CONTRADICT something you already accepted. A contradiction is the moment a pile of documents would have stayed quiet. Finding it is what you are paying the review time for." ;
    kpred:constraint    "IT IS GENUINELY SLOW AT THE START, and this page will not pretend otherwise. A graph of nine facts answers nothing worth asking, and getting past that costs you review time before it returns anything. If you want a tool that reads your files and answers confidently without asking, that exists and this is not it." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI ;
    skos:related        feat:TrustSystem ;
    skos:related        feat:Compare .

feat:TrustSystem
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Source Trust System" ;
    skos:definition     "Sources accumulate trust scores based on your review decisions. Trusted sources can be auto-confirmed. A time-decay formula prevents stale trust from persisting." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI .

feat:Graph3D
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "3D / 2D Knowledge Graph" ;
    skos:definition     "Interactive force-directed graph (a map where linked facts pull toward each other and unrelated ones drift apart) in WebGL (3D) or Canvas (2D fallback). Per-entity icons from urn:kbase:predicate/icon2d statements with emoji fallback. Label overlap prevention sorts by degree and hides collisions. Hub emphasis, layout modes (force/focus/source/type/hub), filter chips." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI ;
    skos:note           "Fixed known issue (PR #21): production builds rendered a black graph while hit-testing still worked. Root cause was Threlte's <T.BufferAttribute> resolving its THREE class by function name, which minification mangled, so the geometry got the class instead of an instance and the WebGL renderer threw every frame. A minified-build deploy gate (PR #24, tests/e2e/graph-render.test.ts + playwright.smoke.config.ts) now guards against regressions, since the bug only reproduces in a minified production build, never under `vite dev`." .

feat:Shelly
    rdf:type            ktype:Person ;
    rdfs:label          "Shelly (AI Assistant)" ;
    skos:definition     "The turtle-shaped AI assistant. Three tabs: tutorial, chat (grounded in your graph), and explore (guided story tours). Each graph can embed its own Shelly persona via the shelly: vocabulary. Supports Whisper STT voice input and Kokoro TTS voice output." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI ;
    skos:related        feat:WhisperSTT ;
    skos:related        feat:KokoroTTS .

feat:Reckoning
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdf:type            skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "A reckoning — asking your graph what to do" ;
    skos:altLabel       "Reckoning" ;
    skos:altLabel       "Situation-Target-Proposal" ;
    skos:altLabel       "STP" ;
    skos:definition     "A reckoning is what this product is named after, and it is the thing you do once you have a graph worth asking. You describe the situation you are in and the outcome you want, and you get back options that are built ONLY from facts you have confirmed — each one showing which of your own facts it stands on. The name is the old sense of the word: to reckon is to work something out from what you have, and to be held to account for the answer." ;
    kpred:page-section  "Learn" ;
    kpred:page-slug     "reckoning" ;
    kpred:audience      "Anyone who has been told this is a note-taking tool and is wondering what it is actually for." ;
    kpred:example       "SITUATION: our staging deploys have failed twice this month, both times after a dependency bump, and the person who set the pipeline up has left. TARGET: stop the failures without rebuilding the pipeline. What comes back is a handful of options assembled from what your graph already holds about those deploys — and each one names the facts it used, so you can see whether it read the situation correctly before you act on it." ;
    kpred:principle     "IT CANNOT USE A FACT YOU HAVE NOT ACCEPTED. That is the entire difference between this and asking a chatbot the same question. A chatbot answers from everything it has ever read plus whatever you pasted; a reckoning answers from your confirmed graph and nothing else, which is why it can tell you it does not have enough to go on instead of producing a confident answer built on nothing." ;
    kpred:principle     "EVERY OPTION SHOWS ITS WORKING. An option that cites three facts you recognise is one you can act on. An option that cites a fact you had forgotten is the useful kind of surprise. An option that cites nothing does not appear, because a proposal with no grounding is a guess with better formatting." ;
    kpred:constraint    "IT IS ONLY AS GOOD AS WHAT YOU PUT IN, and the product does not pretend otherwise. A reckoning over a graph of nine facts will say very little of value. This is the honest cost of the approach: the review step that makes the answer trustworthy is also the step that makes it slow to get started, and no amount of automation removes it without also removing the reason to believe the result." ;
    kpred:constraint    "IT PROPOSES. YOU DECIDE. A reckoning does not act, schedule, send, or change anything — it hands you options and the reasoning behind them. Nothing in this product does something on your behalf because it concluded that it should." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI .


## =============================================================================
## Comparison & Diff
## =============================================================================

feat:Compare
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdf:type            skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Compare — finding out where two people actually disagree" ;
    skos:altLabel       "Compare / Diff Engine" ;
    skos:definition     "Put two graphs side by side and see what is new, what agrees, and what conflicts. Two colleagues who have each read around the same subject can find the three things they disagree about in a minute, instead of discovering them in a meeting six weeks later." ;
    kpred:audience      "Anyone who has to reconcile what they know with what somebody else knows." ;
    kpred:principle     "THIS IS WHAT CLEAR CLAIMS BUY YOU, and it is the return on the review work that is hardest to see up front. Two piles of notes cannot be compared at all — you would have to read both. Two graphs can, but only where they say things in the same shape and the same words. Every time you confirm a fact rather than leaving a paragraph, you are making your knowledge comparable to somebody else\u0027s." ;
    kpred:principle     "AND A STANDARD VOCABULARY IS WHAT MAKES IT FAST. Writing skos:broader instead of inventing your own word for is-part-of is dull and slightly awkward by hand — and it is why a stranger\u0027s graph lines up with yours without a mapping exercise. The tedious part of interoperability is the part you are doing when you pick a standard term." ;
    kpred:principle     "YOU COMPARE CLAIMS, NOT SOURCES. My graph learned something from my file and yours learned it from yours; that is not a disagreement about anything, and a diff that reports it is wasting your attention. What two graphs can genuinely disagree about is what they believe — which is why the relation layers separate a claim about the world from a record of where it came from." ;
    kpred:constraint    "IT CAN ONLY COMPARE WHAT WAS WRITTEN DOWN. Two graphs that use different words for the same relation will show as disagreeing when they agree, and the fix is the same vocabulary work as everywhere else here — there is no clever matching that removes it." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI ;
    skos:related        feat:DiffSummary ;
    skos:related        feat:ReviewSystem .

feat:DiffSummary
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Diff Summary" ;
    skos:definition     "LLM-generated 3-part summaries of diffs: what is new, what reinforces existing knowledge, and what conflicts. Integrated in /compare, /review, and the browser extension sidepanel." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Compare .


## =============================================================================
## Multi-KB & Navigation
## =============================================================================

feat:MultiKB
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Multi-Graph Management" ;
    skos:definition     "Create, switch, rename, and delete independent knowledge graphs. Each graph has its own IndexedDB store, stable UUID, content fingerprint, and optional accent color. Per-tab graph support via URL ?kb= parameter." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI ;
    skos:related        feat:KBLeap .

feat:KBLeap
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Graph Leap" ;
    skos:definition     "Cross-reference entities between graphs. A leap node stores a target (Graph stable ID, app path, or URL) as an ordinary RDF triple. Nodes with leaps show an amber ring. Click to jump to the target graph, navigate within the app, or open an external URL. Docs sub-graphs auto-import on first click." ;
    skos:broader        feat:MultiKB .

feat:KBIdentity
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Graph Identity" ;
    skos:definition     "Each graph has a stable UUID (never changes, used for MCP routing and graph Leap) and a content fingerprint (SHA-256 of sorted N-Quads, changes with every edit). Both visible in Settings." ;
    skos:broader        feat:MultiKB .


## =============================================================================
## Knowledge Management
## =============================================================================

feat:PredicateManager
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Predicate Manager" ;
    skos:definition     "View all predicates in your graph with usage counts. Rename predicates across all statements or merge two predicates into one. Accessible from the graph page." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI .

feat:Disambiguation
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Disambiguation" ;
    skos:definition     "Automatic detection of duplicate or similar entities using text embeddings and cosine similarity. Suggests merges for your review in the Merges tab." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI .

feat:EntityTypes
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Entity Type System" ;
    skos:definition     "Categorize entities (Person, Place, Concept, Tool, Document, Organization, Event) with custom colors and 3D shapes. Types assigned via rdf:type statements." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI .

feat:HistoryMode
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "History Mode" ;
    skos:definition     "Time-travel through your graph at /history. Scrub a timeline to see the graph at any past point. All mutations are logged in the changelog." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI .


## =============================================================================
## LLM & Voice
## =============================================================================

feat:LLMBackends
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "LLM Backends" ;
    skos:definition     "9 providers: Claude, OpenAI, Gemini, Ollama (local), OpenRouter (free tier), WASM (offline, Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct), Chrome AI (Gemini Nano), Manual paste, Mock. Per-task backend overrides let you use different providers for ingest, chat, analysis, diff summary, and merge analysis. Prefer-local routing can redirect chat, diff summary, and merge analysis to a reachable local Ollama server." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI .

feat:WhisperSTT
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Whisper STT" ;
    skos:definition     "Local speech-to-text via transformers.js using whisper-tiny (42MB). Mic button in the chat tab. Runs entirely in-browser -- no cloud, no API key." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Shelly .

feat:KokoroTTS
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Kokoro TTS" ;
    skos:definition     "Local text-to-speech for story walkthroughs. 82M model cached in browser. Falls back to browser speech synthesis if unavailable." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Shelly .


## =============================================================================
## Safety & Export
## =============================================================================

feat:ContentSafety
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Content Safety" ;
    skos:definition     "An ethics preamble is added to the prompts where someone other than you may read the result: always when sharing, and for conversation with a remote model. Private local use and prompts whose every written statement is already checked by the classifier go without it. Content classifier with two levels: blocked (filtered out on ingest) and mature (flagged on export with advisory). Discourse, disagreement, and academic content pass freely." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI .

feat:PassageGrounding
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Passage Grounding" ;
    skos:definition     "Verbatim source excerpts attached to extracted triples. LLM prompt rule requests the exact source sentence. Persists via meta:excerpt in TTL reification. Displayed in StatementCard and DiffEntry." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI .

feat:TTLExport
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Turtle Export" ;
    skos:definition     "Export your graph as a .ttl file with full reification metadata (status, source, confidence, timestamps, excerpts). Roundtrip-safe. Clean export for interop or full export with all metadata." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI .

feat:ModelCache
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Model Cache Management" ;
    skos:definition     "Inspect, sideload, and purge locally cached WASM models. Manifests for Qwen2.5-0.5B (500MB), BGE-small-en-v1.5 (33MB), MiniLM-L6-v2 (22MB), Kokoro 82M (88MB), Whisper Tiny (42MB). Settings > Integrations > local model cache." ;
    skos:broader        feat:LLMBackends .


## =============================================================================
## Cross-KB & Alignment
## =============================================================================

feat:CrossKBAlignment
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Cross-Graph Alignment" ;
    skos:definition     "Align entities across knowledge graphs. Entity matching via exact IRI match and embedding similarity. IRI remapping for entities that represent the same concept across graphs. Align tab in review page. KbPicker selects source graph, AlignmentCard shows each match with accept/reject." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI .

feat:SourceRefresh
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Source Refresh" ;
    skos:definition     "Generic refresh for url, repository, and calendar sources. Auto-refresh on open and on interval (configurable). Graph page refresh button. MCP tools: kb_list_sources and kb_request_refresh. Delta comparison shows what changed since last ingest." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI .

feat:SourceMonitoring
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Source Monitoring" ;
    skos:definition     "Watch URLs for content changes. The n8n Source Monitor workflow checks every 6 hours, detects diffs via content hash, and queues pending notes for review. Surfaces via /webhook/reckons-kb-pending endpoint." ;
    skos:broader        feat:SourceRefresh .


## =============================================================================
## Context Compression
## =============================================================================

feat:ContextCompression
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Context Compression" ;
    skos:definition     "Condense your context. Keep the meaning. Knowledge graphs are dense by nature — a page of prose becomes a handful of triples. Semantic meaning preserved, tokens reduced. Feed compressed graph directly to AI agents via MCP (Model Context Protocol, a standard way for AI assistants to reach tools and data). Structured triples outperform summaries because no relationships are paraphrased away." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI ;
    kpred:has-tool      "kb_compress" ;
    kpred:has-param     "query (required) — topic to extract context for" ;
    kpred:has-param     "budget (default 2000, max 8000) — approximate token budget" ;
    kpred:has-param     "hops (default 1, max 2) — neighborhood expansion depth" ;
    kpred:has-param     "kb — target a specific knowledge graph" .


## =============================================================================
## Entity Normalisation
## =============================================================================

feat:EntityNormalization
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Entity Normalization" ;
    skos:definition     "Post-extraction normalization that rewrites incoming IRIs to match existing graph entities and predicates using embedding similarity. Prevents duplicate entities like 'octopus-vulgaris' vs 'common-octopus' from entering the review queue. Two-pass matching: exact label (case-insensitive) then cosine similarity (0.90 entity, 0.88 predicate). Protected standard vocabularies (rdf:, rdfs:, skos:, xsd:) are never remapped." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Ingest ;
    skos:related        feat:Disambiguation .


## =============================================================================
## MCP Workspace (Self-Dogfooding)
## =============================================================================

feat:MCPWorkspace
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "MCP Workspace" ;
    skos:definition     "Reckons.AI uses its own MCP server to track product state. Three internal graphs (Roadmap, Production, Features) are symlinked from static/*.ttl into mcp-workspace/kbs/. Claude Code queries these graphs before planning work. Edit a TTL file and the MCP server auto-reloads. Setup: bash scripts/setup-mcp-workspace.sh." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI .


## =============================================================================
## Local jobs over MCP (F267)
## =============================================================================

feat:LocalJobsMCP
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Local jobs through MCP" ;
    skos:definition     "Long-running work (an e2e suite, a benchmark, a research or batch-analysis run) belongs on the local job queue, not inline on a cloud model. Three tools on the MCP server reach that queue: kb_job_list shows every defined job with its tier, what it does and its last run; kb_job_enqueue(name, args?) queues one by name and returns a queue id; kb_job_status(id or name) reports queued, running, done or failed with the exit code, the last 40 log lines and where the full log is. Only jobs defined in static/reckons-jobs.ttl or the schedules file can be queued, never a command. Queueing works with no worker running: the job waits until npm run agent:watch starts the worker. Not built yet: a hook that stops a foreground long-running command and names these tools, and defining a job in the app UI." ;
    skos:broader        feat:MCPWorkspace .


## =============================================================================
## Confluence Migration (F26)
## =============================================================================

feat:ConfluenceMigration
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Confluence Migration" ;
    skos:definition     "Bulk import from Confluence spaces. Upload an HTML export ZIP, parse the page tree, chunk large pages with sliding window (10K chars, 2K overlap), and extract triples using local models (Ollama recommended). Preserves page hierarchy as skos:broader, converts Confluence labels to entity types, tracks provenance per page. Pause/resume checkpoint for overnight imports of large spaces." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Ingest ;
    skos:related        feat:EntityNormalization .

feat:TextChunking
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Text Chunking" ;
    skos:definition     "A long document is too big to read in one pass, so Reckons.AI reads it in overlapping pieces of about 12,000 characters. Each piece is labelled with the document title and its place (piece 3 of 8, for example), so nothing is read out of context. When two pieces produce the same fact, you see it once. This works for every kind of source." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Ingest ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:text-chunking .


## =============================================================================
## Stories & Persona
## =============================================================================

feat:StorySystem
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Story System" ;
    skos:definition     "Guided walkthroughs defined as triples using the story: vocabulary. Steps can highlight entities, trigger prompts, and pose questions. Playback with countdown timer and TTS. Shareable via .ttl files. The Generated assets example also offers an interactive Create an asset with Shelly story: describe an image, wait for local generation, then choose video, Gaussian splat or another image. The story waits for your choices and checked results instead of auto-advancing. Previously imported Generated assets examples get the same story entry." ;
    kpred:description "Local generation is optional. Start npm run gen:serve -- --workspace=/path/to/private-workspace on the machine with the tools installed by scripts/content/gen-setup.sh, then connect that same folder in the story (desktop Chrome or Edge). The runner advertises installed image, video and splat tools; missing tools are shown as unavailable. A browser without workspace folder access cannot dispatch these jobs. No model downloads or jobs start merely from opening a story." ;
    kpred:description "Requests and receipts live in .reckons-generation inside the private workspace. Each job has a stable ID; reopening the story resumes it. Generation writes new assets, never overwrites an example, and adds checked outputs as pending facts with prompt, model, seed, license, time and source-image links. The browser verifies file hashes before attachment. Cancelling prevents attachment; failed images cannot feed derivations. Done for now exits the story, and an active job continues when the panel closes. General free-form chat dispatch and arbitrary derivation tools are not implemented." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Shelly .

feat:TurtlePersona
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Persona System" ;
    skos:definition     "Each graph can embed its own AI assistant personality using the shelly: vocabulary. A work graph might have a direct, technical persona while a personal graph has a calm guide. Persona travels with the .ttl file." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Shelly .


## =============================================================================
## Git Analysis (F24)
## =============================================================================

feat:GitAnalysis
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    kpred:test-coverage "none" ;
    kpred:honest-note   "No automated test covers this (audited 2026-07-12). Shipped and in use; the coverage gap is declared rather than hidden (kb:honest-status). NOTE: this entity duplicates kb:git-analysis in the roadmap under a different IRI — same feature, split identity." ;
    rdfs:label          "Git Analysis" ;
    skos:definition     "Git-aware MCP tools for agent plan alignment. Tools: kb_git_status (branch/commits), kb_check_plan (BM25 drift detection), kb_pending (review queue), kb_git_diff_triples (file-to-Graph cross-ref), kb_alignment_score (quantitative 0-1 score across 4 dimensions: coverage, status alignment, dependency respect, scope discipline). Enhanced kb_add_note supports type, priority, agent, and commit_sha metadata." ;
    skos:broader        feat:MCPWorkspace .


## =============================================================================
## Currents & Pod View (F29)
## =============================================================================

feat:Currents
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Currents" ;
    skos:definition     "Currents brings recurring external content into a space through your optional n8n Currents Monitor. Configure streams and an entity-type gate on the space management page, then explicitly connect Currents in Integrations. While the app is open, it sends saved current definitions and the space ID to your instance and checks for arrivals every five minutes. New document metadata enters the review queue as pending facts; repeated delivery preserves your decisions. Removing or disabling a current stops its remote schedule on the next successful sync. Disconnecting the browser or changing instances leaves existing server schedules running. The monitor workflow must be installed and active, allow this app through CORS, and be permitted by the build connection policy. A custom server needs a self-hosted build configured for its origin. Automatic ranking, direct RSS fallback and full-text extraction are not connected to this delivery path yet." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI ;
    skos:related        feat:PodView ;
    kpred:honest-note   "WITHOUT A CONNECTED SERVER NOTHING ARRIVES. A current saved without n8n connected delivers nothing, and the currents section on the Spaces page says so. Delivery was not tested against a live n8n server before v0.2.6." ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:currents ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:currents-client-wiring .

feat:PodView
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Pod View" ;
    skos:definition     "Turn on the pod view from the Graph tab (currents section, where you manage feeds that bring new facts in) to see arrivals -- nodes touched only by pending facts from a current -- drift gently at reduced opacity with a dashed halo, kept visually distinct until you accept or dismiss them from the node. Accept folds an arrival into the graph for normal review; dismiss clears it. It is a per-device view preference (stored locally, not in the graph) and is honoured by the home graph view." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Currents ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:currents-phase-3 .

## =============================================================================
## Review Workbench (F30)
## =============================================================================

feat:ReviewWorkbench
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Review Workbench" ;
    skos:definition     "The review page pairs its four tabs (incoming, deletions, merges, align) with a preview graph (a small live map beside the review list): browse controls step through items, a node-details + chat pane explains the selected fact in context, and clicking a review item flies the preview graph to its node with the relevant edge highlighted." ;
    skos:broader        feat:ReviewSystem .

feat:QuestionPresettle
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:question-presettle ;
    rdfs:label          "Open questions answered from what you already decided" ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:question-presettle ;
    skos:definition     "An open question in the review queue — one with no proposed answer — can only be rejected or put off, so it waits. Before it does, a job tries to answer it from your own knowledge base. First a rule: when the question is about a file that may have been deleted, the job requires evidence of its deletion in dev history before offering an answer saying the question no longer applies. Absence from one checkout alone is not enough. Then a model running on your machine, with no call to the cloud, may answer, but it may only quote a decision you already recorded, and a second model on your machine checks the quote is really there before the answer is offered. An answer arrives as a proposal attached to the question's own decision: accepting it settles the question in one act, and the job itself settles nothing — it writes proposals, never decisions. In the run recorded on 2026-10-29 (UTC), 232 open questions yielded three candidate answers after the checks; human review found two usable, and only those two were queued. The checks also refused eleven model answers. The job did not settle any decision itself. It is a tool you run before asking, not a recurring job: at that yield, the queue mostly asks for an action, not a fact." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "MOST OF THE QUEUE IS NOT ANSWERABLE THIS WAY. The questions the run left open are mostly machine findings that want an action, and the rest are genuinely open. The checking model was calibrated on two cases, and its precision on that one run was 2 of 3. An inline answer box on the review page is a later part of the same plan, and is not this job." ;
    skos:broader        feat:ReviewSystem .

## =============================================================================
## Published Docs Site (F27)
## =============================================================================

feat:PublishedDocs
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Published Graph Site" ;
    skos:definition     "Any graph can publish itself as a browsable website. Entities typed as a web page export to markdown with frontmatter (title, section, order, excerpt) via the graph's own structure: the parent-child links decide the section a page sits in, and the ordering facts decide the sequence pages read in. The generated site is served at /docs. The graph stays the source of truth: generated pages are regenerated from the graph, and hand-edits to them are overwritten by the next regeneration by design. A Git-backed admin UI edits non-generated content (like release posts); a drift check flags generated pages that no longer match their graph." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI ;
    skos:related        feat:ReleaseNotes .

feat:ReleaseNotes
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Release Notes" ;
    skos:definition     "Versioned release posts (starting with v0.1.0) authored as graph facts and published through the docs site, using the same web-page model as the rest of /docs." ;
    skos:broader        feat:PublishedDocs .

## =============================================================================
## Prefer-Local Routing (Local Offload)
## =============================================================================

feat:PreferLocal
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Prefer-Local Routing" ;
    skos:definition     "Opt-in setting that redirects chat, diff-summary, and merge-analysis to a local Ollama model whenever it is reachable, instead of your chosen cloud backend. Falls back to your normal backend chain the moment Ollama is unreachable -- no extraction quality is sacrificed silently. A companion structured-extraction mode uses a compact, schema-constrained prompt so small local models still produce clean facts." ;
    skos:broader        feat:LLMBackends .

