## =============================================================================
## Adding things, and where a fact came from (v0.2.5)
## =============================================================================

feat:AddMenu
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "The add menu" ;
    skos:definition     "The + button at the top of every page opens a short menu of what you can add. Under New: a source (a whole document), a single statement, a set or a new space. Under From: a note, a link, a folder, a code repository, a calendar, a reminder or a space file. Each choice opens the right page already set up for it. The arrow keys move through the menu and Escape closes it." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Ingest ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:add-quick-menu ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:add-quick-menu .

feat:NodeSources
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label          "Where a node's facts came from" ;
    skos:definition     "Select a node and its details now show which sources its facts came from. Under each source are the facts that source gave, so you can see at a glance what came from where. Click a source's title to open it in Sources, where it is selected for you and any filter that would hide it is cleared. Facts are listed whichever direction they point, even when the Statements filters would hide them." ;
    skos:broader        feat:PassageGrounding ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:statement-node-source-links ;
    skos:broadMatch     kb:source-atoms ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:statement-node-source-links ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:source-atoms .

## =============================================================================
## The evidence chain: a fact points at the words it came from (v0.2.6)
## =============================================================================

feat:OpenInSource
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:statement-to-passage-jump ;
    rdfs:label          "Open a fact's source at the exact words" ;
    skos:definition     "When Reckons.AI reads a source and proposes a fact, the fact records exactly which stretch of text it came from: the passage, where the quote starts and ends, and a fingerprint of the source's text at that moment. Open a fact's Evidence and press Open in source: the source opens, scrolls to the passage, and the quoted words are marked. If the quote cannot be found in the text that was kept, there is no button, and the Evidence section says why. If the source's text has changed since the fact was taken from it, the evidence is marked stale, either because the source changed or because the passage moved. A stale link is never quietly pointed at different words." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "TEXT ONLY. A fact can point at a passage of text, not at a page of a PDF, a box on a page or a region of an image. Those kinds of anchor are planned and not built." ;
    skos:broader        feat:PassageGrounding ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:statement-to-passage-jump ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:anchor-contract ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:anchor-staleness .

feat:SpaceFilePassages
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:chunk-space-folder ;
    rdfs:label          "A space file that carries its evidence" ;
    skos:definition     "A space's file now holds the passages its facts came from, in their own section after the facts. Each fact's link to its words is written too, in the W3C Web Annotation form, so other tools can read it. Copy that one file to another computer or another person, import it, and Open in source still lands on the same words. Saving, sync, Google Drive backups, auto-save and export all write the passages; an import checks each passage against its fingerprint and never overwrites one that is already there. Files from before this change still import as they did." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "ORIGINALS STAY BESIDE THE FILE. The passages travel inside the space's file; the original documents they were cut from are still kept next to it, not inside the space's folder. Moving them is planned." ;
    skos:broader        feat:TTLExport ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:chunk-importer-parse ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:chunk-section-export ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:chunk-space-folder ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:anchor-roundtrip .

feat:Proposals
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:broadMatch     kb:graph-proposals ;
    rdfs:label          "Proposals: decisions that cite their evidence" ;
    skos:definition     "A proposal is how Reckons.AI will put a decision in front of you: two to four options, each listing the facts it rests on, and through those facts the passages they came from, so you can follow any option back to its evidence before you choose. The shape of a proposal is now defined and checked: what a valid one must contain, the reasons an invalid one is refused, and the stages it moves through from suggested to decided." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "A DEFINITION ONLY. Nothing in the app makes, stores or shows a proposal yet. Building the options with their citations, and the screen to review them, are planned." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:proposal-shape .

feat:StarterReviewed
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:starter-provenance ;
    rdfs:label          "Getting started arrives already reviewed" ;
    skos:definition     "The Getting started space is a small made-up story: two friends, Alex and Jordan, planning a camping weekend. It comes with seven short sample sources (a text thread, a voice memo, notes on each campground, a forecast and route notes), each opening with a line that says it is a sample. Its 48 facts each show the passage they came from, who accepted them and when, including two corrections and two rejections, so the review history you see is a real one. Three facts are left for you: the Review tab opens with them waiting for your decision." ;
    skos:broader        guide:WhatIsReckonsAI ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:starter-provenance .

## =============================================================================
## Spaces you can stand inside (v0.2.6)
## =============================================================================

feat:Splats
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:splat-node-view ;
    rdfs:label          "3D captures on a node" ;
    skos:definition     "A node can carry a Gaussian splat: a 3D object or scene drawn as many small, soft points of color. In the 3D graph the splat is drawn at the node, and the nodes, lines and labels behind it are hidden, so it reads as a solid thing. Open it in the viewer to turn it and look from any side. Pictures, clips, 3D models and splats are built-in types (Image, Video, 3D model, Gaussian splat), alongside Prompt and License. How an asset was made, and under which license, shows in its details rather than as extra nodes, while the link to what it was made from stays a visible line." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "3D ONLY. The 2D view does not draw splats. Hiding what sits behind a splat was measured in software rendering, not on a real graphics card. Image, Video and License share a shape with other types and are told apart by color and icon." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Graph3D ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:splat-node-view ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:splat-occlusion ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:media-entity-types .

feat:SpaceBackground
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:splat-space-background ;
    rdfs:label          "A background for a space" ;
    skos:definition     "A space can have a background behind its 3D graph. Choose Set as background on a node that carries a splat, or import a capture from your device (.ply, .spz or .sog files; it warns above 50 MB and refuses above 300 MB, and the file is stored on your device, never copied into the space's text). Then open View settings, the panel at the top left of the graph: choose whether the background sits behind the graph or all around you, turn it, change its size, position and opacity, or level its floor. Place in space puts a node's splat where you want it inside the background, at the size you want, without the large preview covering the graph. A scene from threejs.org can be a background too: it never loads until you press Load, runs in a sealed frame, and can be stopped from View settings or from the bar on the graph. A space has one background at a time." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "A Three.js scene that crashes the browser's graphics driver can still take the page down; the sealed frame keeps it away from your data, not from the driver." ;
    skos:broader        feat:Graph3D ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:splat-space-background ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:splat-background-settings ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:splat-file-import ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:splat-placement ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:background-in-view ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:threejs-example-background .

feat:YourRoom
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:room-scene-space ;
    rdfs:label          "The Your room example space" ;
    skos:definition     "Your room is an example space that walks through four steps: scan a room, import the capture, generate an object, and place it in the room. It opens from About, under example data. Its room is a reconstruction of a computer-made scene, with a carved turtle placed inside it." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "A GUIDED EXAMPLE, NOT A FINISHED FEATURE. The room is not a real scan, and the placement steps are not covered by a browser test on phones." ;
    skos:broader        feat:SpaceBackground ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:room-scene-space .

feat:RoomScan
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:room-scan-capture ;
    rdfs:label          "Turn a phone scan of a room into a splat, on your own machine" ;
    skos:definition     "With the generation tools installed (see Generation tools and setup), npm run scan:room -- <folder or video> turns a phone recording of a room into a splat: it pulls frames from the video, works out where the camera was for each one, and trains the splat. The result and a receipt stay on your machine, outside the project. npm run scan:quality says how good a recording is (too fast, too shaky, too dark, parts of the room not covered) with one plain tip for each problem. npm run splat:compress makes a splat much smaller in the SPZ or SOG format, checks the copy against the original, and records that the copy is lossy." ;
    kpred:honest-note   "MEASURED ON ONE MACHINE. A 300-frame phone recording took about 19 minutes on one Linux computer with an NVIDIA RTX 3090. It runs on Linux only, the room's scale is arbitrary, and the quality tips use first-guess thresholds from one private scan." ;
    skos:broader        feat:GenerationSetup ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:room-scan-capture ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:scan-quality ;
    kpred:relates-to    kb:splat-compression .

feat:ReckoningSimulator
    rdf:type            ktype:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch     kb:sim-core ;
    rdfs:label          "The reckoning simulator, first part" ;
    skos:definition     "A reckoning will be able to try a proposal in a physical scene before you choose it. The first part reads a scene from the graph, steps it with a physics engine, and checks it against conditions such as 'the ball ends in the cup'. npm run sim:run runs the example in static/starter-sim-ramp-cup.ttl, which fails as written and passes once the ball starts higher on the ramp." ;
