feat:GenerationSetup rdf:type ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Generation tools and setup" ;
    skos:exactMatch kb:generated-assets-story ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:generated-assets-story ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:shelly-generation-jobs ;
    kpred:relates-to kb:gen-setup-by-os ;
    skos:broader feat:StorySystem ;
    skos:definition """The Generated assets story works in two layers. Viewing existing assets is included. Creating new files uses optional tools on your own generation machine.

| Capability | Available with the app | Additional setup |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Explore example images, videos and Gaussian splats | Bundled examples and viewers | None |
| View/import images, videos, GLB models and splats | Browser asset viewers | Your own compatible files |
| Prompt to image | Story interface | stable-diffusion.cpp with Z-Image-Turbo weights and the local runner |
| Image to video | Story interface | stable-diffusion.cpp with Wan2.2 TI2V-5B weights and the local runner |
| Image to Gaussian splat | Story interface | TripoSplat, Python/CUDA dependencies and the local runner |
| Generate GLB meshes or other derivations | Not implemented in this story | A future adapter; importing/viewing GLB does not generate one |
| Install tools/plugins through an agent | No installer bundled in the story | An external agent you configure and explicitly authorize |

### Set up local generation

This takes about an hour, most of it waiting for downloads. You need a terminal (the command-line window: Terminal on a Mac, any terminal on Linux), about 30 GB of free disk space, and desktop Chrome or Edge. Safari and Firefox cannot connect a folder, which the story needs.

**What runs where.** The browser only asks for a picture, clip or splat. A small program on your own computer, the runner, does the work with open models and writes the result into a folder you choose. Nothing is sent to a cloud service.

| Your computer | Pictures | Video clips | Splats from a picture | Room scans | Tested |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Linux, x86-64, NVIDIA GPU with 24 GB | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes, Ubuntu 24.04 with RTX 3090s |
| Mac with Apple Silicon (M1 or later) | yes | yes, slow | **no**: the splat model needs an NVIDIA GPU | yes | **no, not yet run on a Mac** |
| Intel Mac | no | no | no | no | not supported |
| Windows | no | no | no | no | not supported; Linux inside WSL2 has never been tried |

**On a Mac, read this first.** The Mac steps are prepared but nobody has run them on a Mac yet, so expect rough edges and please report what happens. Video needs a lot of memory: on an NVIDIA card a clip used about 17 GB, and on a Mac the same memory also serves every other app, so a Mac with 16 GB is very likely too small for video. 32 GB or more is the safer starting point. These Mac numbers are estimates, not measurements.

#### Step 1. Install the basics

On a **Mac**: open Terminal and install [Homebrew](https://brew.sh) by following the one-line instruction on its page. Then run:

```
xcode-select --install
brew install node git uv ffmpeg
```

On **Linux (Ubuntu)**: run `sudo apt install git curl unzip ffmpeg`, install the JavaScript runtime at version 22 or later (for example with [nvm](https://github.com/nvm-sh/nvm)), and install [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/). Install the NVIDIA driver for your card if it is not already there.

#### Step 2. Get Reckons.AI

```
git clone https://github.com/Data-Insight-Solutions/Reckons.AI.git
cd Reckons.AI
npm ci
```

#### Step 3. Install the generation tools and models

Read [the setup script](https://github.com/Data-Insight-Solutions/Reckons.AI/blob/dev/scripts/content/gen-setup.sh) first; it is short. Then run `bash scripts/content/gen-setup.sh`. It checks your system, says what is missing and how to install it, and stops before downloading anything it cannot use. It installs into `~/.local/share/reckons/gen`, outside the Reckons.AI folder; set `RECKONS_GEN_HOME` to choose another place and use the same value when you start the runner. The programs it downloads from GitHub are checked against pinned fingerprints (SHA-256); the model files from Hugging Face are not checked yet. If it stops part-way, run it again: it resumes and skips what is already there.

On a Mac it installs the Apple Silicon (Metal) build of stable-diffusion.cpp for pictures and video, and Brush and COLMAP for room scans, and skips the splat model, about 4.5 GB less. On Linux it installs the Vulkan build, the CUDA version of PyTorch and the splat model as well.

#### Step 4. Start the runner

Make an empty folder for your generated files, outside the Reckons.AI folder (for example `~/Documents/reckons-assets`). Then, from the Reckons.AI folder, run:

```
npm run gen:serve -- --no-recurring --workspace="$HOME/Documents/reckons-assets"
```

Leave that terminal open; the runner works only while it runs. It takes one request at a time and writes each result with a private receipt, a saved record of how the file was made and checked. `--no-recurring` makes it run only what you ask for; without it, it also runs the project's own scheduled maintenance jobs, which are meant for the developers' machines.

#### Step 5. Make something

Open Reckons.AI in desktop Chrome or Edge, open the Generated assets example, choose **Create an asset with Shelly**, and connect the same folder. The runner tells the app which kinds of file this computer can make, and the app offers only those: on a Mac, there is no splat choice. Type what picture you want; when it arrives, choose a video and describe its motion, or (on Linux) a Gaussian splat. A splat invents the sides the picture does not show; it is not a measured scan.

#### If something goes wrong

- *The setup script says something is missing*: install what it names and run it again.
- *A stage says files are missing*: run the setup script again; a download may have been interrupted.
- *A video fails part-way on a Mac*: the computer probably ran out of memory. Close other apps and try again; if it still fails, video needs a Mac with more memory.
- *The app cannot connect the folder*: use desktop Chrome or Edge, not Safari or Firefox.

#### Step 6. Test it and tell us what happened

If you are trying this for us, these four checks are the test. Write down the time each one takes.

1. **A picture.** Ask for something simple, for example *a ceramic teapot on a wooden table, soft daylight*. It should appear within a minute or two.
2. **A video from that picture.** Choose the video option and describe a small movement, for example *the camera slowly circles the teapot*. On a Mac this is the slow, memory-hungry step; let it run.
3. **The splat choice.** On a Mac it should not be offered at all, and the app should say why. On Linux, make one.
4. **Look at the results.** Open each one. Does the picture match the prompt? Does the clip move?

Then send back:

- your computer: chip and memory (on a Mac: Apple menu, About This Mac) and the operating system version;
- which of the four checks worked, and how long each took;
- for anything that failed: which step, and the last 30 or so lines of the terminal window where it failed (the setup script, or the one running `npm run gen:serve`).

Send it to whoever invited you, or open an issue at [github.com/Data-Insight-Solutions/Reckons.AI/issues](https://github.com/Data-Insight-Solutions/Reckons.AI/issues). Nothing you generate leaves your computer unless you choose to send it.

#### Remove everything afterwards

1. Stop the runner: press Ctrl+C in its terminal, or close it.
2. Delete the tools and models, about 25 to 30 GB: `rm -rf ~/.local/share/reckons/gen` (or the folder you chose with `RECKONS_GEN_HOME`).
3. Delete the Reckons.AI folder and your generated-files folder if you no longer want them.
4. Optional, on a Mac: `brew uninstall ffmpeg uv` removes the tools Homebrew installed for this. Leave them if other software uses them.

The connected runner reports which stages have their tool and model files installed. Installed files do not guarantee enough GPU memory or a compatible driver; runtime failures are shown. Prompts, requests, receipts and outputs remain in `.reckons-generation` in the private workspace. Stop the runner with Ctrl+C or by closing its terminal. Closing Shelly leaves an active job running; Cancel generation prevents attachment and asks the runner to stop the process. If a runner was killed without stopping cleanly, the next one to start takes over its lock once it finds that runner is gone; it refuses while another runner is still running on the same workspace.

### What it takes: measured, per stage

Measured on 2x RTX 3090 (24 GB each, one card used per run), driver 575.57.08, Ryzen 9 3900X with 31 GB RAM, Ubuntu 24.04, on 2026-10-20. GPU memory is the peak held by the run's own processes (nvidia-smi), not the whole card. System RAM is the peak resident size of the largest process. Time is wall clock from start to file, and the model-loading part is included.

| Stage | Peak GPU memory | Peak system RAM | Time | Weights to download (on disk) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Image, standard (768 px, 8 steps) | 11,992 MiB (11.7 GiB) | 1.2 GiB | 15.6 s | 11.2 GB (Z-Image-Turbo weights) |
| Image, showcase (1024 px, 8 steps) | 13,258 MiB (12.9 GiB) | 1.2 GiB | 19.5 s | the same files |
| Video (49 frames, 512 px, 20 steps) | 17,356 MiB (16.9 GiB) | 1.5 GiB | 58.8 s | 12.9 GB (Wan2.2 weights) |
| Splat, standard (32,768 Gaussians, 20 steps) | 6,998 MiB (6.8 GiB) | 5.3 GiB | 31.5 s (11.2 s load + 20.3 s generate) | 4.5 GB (TripoSplat weights) |
| Splat, showcase (262,144 Gaussians, 20 steps) | 7,000 MiB (6.8 GiB) | 5.3 GiB | 33.7 s (11.0 s load + 22.7 s generate) | the same files |

All three models' weights together are 28.5 GB. Smaller GPUs are untested: nothing here was run on a card below 24 GB, so no minimum is claimed. The splat stage's peak is about 7 GB at both quality levels, because the Gaussian count only changes how many points the decoder writes out, so the video stage (about 17 GB) is the one a smaller card is most likely to fail. The image numbers include 1024 px because the splat model resizes its input to 1024 px anyway. System RAM was read as the largest single process, so a machine that also runs a browser and other tools needs more than this column alone.

### Tools and integration guides

- [stable-diffusion.cpp](https://github.com/leejet/stable-diffusion.cpp) and [Z-Image-Turbo weights](https://huggingface.co/leejet/Z-Image-Turbo-GGUF).
- [Wan2.2 weights](https://huggingface.co/QuantStack/Wan2.2-TI2V-5B-GGUF).
- [TripoSplat](https://github.com/VAST-AI-Research/TripoSplat).
- [Agent setup and permissions](/docs/features/agent-setup).
- [Integration settings](/settings/integrations), [MCP setup](/docs/integrations-tech/mcp-server), and [Ollama setup](/docs/integrations-tech/ollama).

A local language model does not automatically supply image/video/splat generation. Those are distinct tools with separate model files. The universal integration/plugin SDK and in-app agent management are planned; this guide does not imply that either is available today.""" .

feat:AgentSetup rdf:type ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Agent setup and installation permissions" ;
    kpred:status-not-applicable "A guide to configuring external agents and granting installation permissions, not an in-app installer." ;
    skos:broader feat:StorySystem ;
    skos:definition """An external coding or automation agent can help install generation tools, plugins, or an optional integration when you give it the appropriate terminal or SSH access. Reckons.AI's browser story does not provide that agent, grant access, install services or hold SSH credentials.

### Start with a limited task

1. Configure your chosen agent using its own setup instructions. If it should read Reckons.AI graphs, connect [the MCP server](/docs/integrations-tech/mcp-server). Reading a graph is separate from permission to run its commands.
2. Give it the specific installation guide and desired result: for example, install the local image/splat tools, configure your own Indico calendar service, or connect your own n8n webhook. Ask for an inspectable plan naming downloads, licenses, disk/GPU requirements, configuration changes, ports and rollback steps.
3. Approve only the necessary effect on the chosen device or server. **Warning: terminal/device control can read or change accessible files, run downloaded code and install software. SSH access extends that authority to the remote account. Elevated privileges can affect the whole machine.** Use a dedicated account with limited permissions, keep backups, and never put passwords, private keys or tokens in a shared graph or prompt example.
4. Let the authorized agent perform the approved installation, then verify the result independently: a checked generated image, a successful read from the configured calendar, or a synthetic webhook test. Review logs and failures. Revoke temporary credentials and remove unneeded privileges when finished.

Reckons.AI includes a developer task runner at `scripts/agent/runner.ts`. For a trusted local task graph, `npx tsx scripts/agent/runner.ts --graph=/path/to/tasks.ttl --dry-run` shows planned work. Its default declared authority is read-only; broader effects must be allowed explicitly. Effect declarations are not an operating-system sandbox. Do not run task graphs supplied by strangers or grant broad effects merely to bypass a failure. The dedicated generation runner is narrower: it accepts fixed generation stages, not graph-authored shell commands.

### Optional service setup

[Indico installation](https://docs.getindico.io/en/stable/installation/) and [n8n hosting](https://docs.n8n.io/deploy/host-n8n/) are service-specific guides. An authorized external agent with appropriate SSH access can follow them on a server you control; server provisioning is not a built-in Reckons.AI feature. Connect the resulting service through [Integrations settings](/settings/integrations). These services are optional and per-user. Reckons.AI remains usable without either server.

For model jobs, see [the local agent tier](/docs/coding-workflow/agent-tier). The broader [portable orchestration design](/docs/coding-workflow/agent-orchestration) and in-app agent dashboard remain planned; they are not prerequisites for an external agent helping you with an approved install.""" .

feat:ThreeExamples rdf:type ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Three.js examples on nodes" ;
    kpred:page-slug "threejs-examples" ;
    skos:exactMatch kb:threejs-example-nodes ;
    skos:broader feat:StorySystem ;
    skos:definition """Attach an interactive example from [the Three.js gallery](https://threejs.org/examples/) to a node. This opens a separate viewer; it does not turn the example's scene into geometry inside the graph.

1. Select an entity node and find **Three.js example** in its details.
2. Choose **Attach Three.js example**, paste a gallery link such as `https://threejs.org/examples/#webgl_animation_keyframes`, then choose **Attach example**. Direct `https://threejs.org/examples/webgl_animation_keyframes.html` links also work. Only official HTTPS WebGL and WebGPU example links are accepted; XR, arbitrary websites, query options and pasted JavaScript are not supported.
3. Choose **Open example viewer**, then **Load example**. Opening a space, selecting the node or importing its attachment does not execute the example. The explicit load contacts threejs.org and runs its third-party code on your graphics hardware.
4. Use **Stop example** or close the viewer to unload it. A failed example offers **Retry example**. Editing or removing the attachment is available in node details. An attached example takes precedence over that node's other asset previews until removed.

### As the graph background

With an example attached, choose **Set as background** in node details. A bar then appears in the graph view reading "Background: <example name>" with **Load**. Nothing starts until you choose Load, including after a reload or when you open an imported graph. Once running, the bar offers **Interact with background** (sends mouse and touch input to the example until you press the button again or Escape; Escape does not work while the example itself has keyboard focus, so use the button) and **Stop**. A failed background unloads and the bar shows the error and **Retry**. A space has one background: setting an example replaces a background splat, and setting a splat replaces the example.

The background is a separate scene. It does not share the graph's camera, depth or lighting, so nodes float in front of it instead of sitting inside it, and it does not follow the graph camera. It runs a second scene beside the graph, which roughly adds its GPU load to the graph's; stop it on a slow device. The same sandbox and failure-containment limits below apply.

### Loading protection and its limits

The viewer uses a sandboxed frame without same-origin privileges, forms, popups or top-level navigation. The example cannot read Reckons.AI's document or browser storage. Camera, microphone, location, clipboard, fullscreen and XR permissions are not granted. The frame allows scripts and assets from threejs.org; examples requiring other origins or permissions may fail.

Before downloading the example, the frame checks for graphics API availability. It reports script errors, failed asset loads, rejected promises and lost graphics contexts. The parent unloads a scene that has not submitted a GPU draw or WebGPU queue submission within 45 seconds, or whose heartbeat stops for 10 seconds while the app is visible. A document load alone is not success. GPU activity is not a guarantee of correct appearance; examples can still render incorrectly or use too much memory.

**This is failure containment, not guaranteed crash protection.** An iframe is not a dedicated operating-system process. A severe browser, shared rendering-thread or GPU-driver failure can still freeze or crash the tab before its watchdog runs. Use a lighter example if your device struggles. Example compatibility and upstream contents can change.

No local models, plugins or SSH setup are needed for these viewers. They require internet access to threejs.org and compatible browser graphics support. This does not generate or copy an asset into your space; the saved node metadata holds a link. See the [Three.js compatibility guide](https://threejs.org/manual/pages/webgl-compatibility-check.html), and [generation setup](/docs/features/generation-setup) for creating your own image, video or splat.""" .


## =============================================================================
## Reckoning simulator (F260), phase 1 only
## =============================================================================

feat:SimCore rdf:type ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Physical scene simulator (early, command line only)" ;
    kpred:page-slug "physical-scene-simulator" ;
    skos:exactMatch kb:reckoning-simulator ;
    skos:broader feat:StorySystem ;
    skos:definition """A collection of facts can describe a small physical scene, and a command checks whether a goal comes true in it. This is the first step of a larger plan and, today, it is a developer tool: it has no screen in the app, no replay, and the reckoning does not use it yet.

Each item with a shape (a box, sphere or capsule) is a rigid body with a mass, friction and bounciness, and a start position taken from the item's placement setting. A target item lists goals in a small fixed vocabulary, such as `inside(ball, cup-zone)` or `aboveFloor(all, -0.1)`. A goal that is misspelled or names a body that does not exist is reported and never run.

To try it, run `npm run sim:run -- --file=static/starter-sim-ramp-cup.ttl`. The command simulates 10 seconds, prints PASS or FAIL for each goal with the final position of every moving body, and exits with an error code if any goal fails. The example ball, as given, starts too low on the ramp to clear the cup's lip, so the run fails; moving it higher up the ramp makes the same run pass.

The physics engine is Rapier. The same run with the same inputs gives the same positions on the same build, which is tested; this is not a promise across different builds or browsers. Runs are limited in length and in the number of bodies. Later phases are planned to let a reckoning propose changes to a scene and test each one here, and to replay a run in the 3D view.""" .

feat:TripleCheck a ktype:Concept, skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Triple Check" ; skos:broader guide:WhatIsReckonsAI ;
    skos:definition "Optional continuous checking of statements against your other spaces and, if you allow it, cited web pages. What it finds arrives in Review as pending statements from a new source." ;
    kpred:description "Open Settings → Triple Check and enable checking for this space. While the app is open it checks one statement about once a minute. A page or another space that says the same thing becomes the same statement from that source, which you can accept as an extra citation. One that says something different becomes its own statement beside yours. A page that disputes a statement without saying what is true instead becomes a question. Nothing is accepted for you, and a proposal you rejected is not made again. In Review, group by source to accept or reject everything one source added at once; statements that disagree are held back for their own decision. All local spaces are compared, excluding archives. Optional meaning comparison uses your Ollama endpoint. Web checking sends each checked statement to the search service and model you chose, and can be turned off for sensitive content. To try it, open the walkthrough from Settings → Triple Check." ;
    kpred:description "Each check retains dated citations. A likely true or false label summarizes a small sample of distinct publisher passages; it is not proof or a calibrated probability. Copied pages do not establish independence. You may ignore suggestions, mark evidence reviewed, or decide whether to refine a statement. Checking never accepts or rewrites your assertions automatically." ;
    kpred:description "Repository comparison views show a selected shortlist, dated GitHub metrics and cited qualitative proposals. Save selected entities, relationships or specific assertions as a space view. The canvas limits visible entities by default and discloses hidden statements; expanding the view changes only the display. Analyze can explicitly apply purpose-based organization with a recovery snapshot. The bundled synthetic research space demonstrates conflicting document corpora." ;
    kpred:honest-note "Evidence is stored in this browser and is not currently exported with the space. Paid provider accuracy and cost have not been calibrated. Enterprise SME discovery, RBAC and approved evidence projections remain planned; local checking provides no enterprise access enforcement." .


